  "markup": "\u003Cp\u003EThis fictional case is for demonstration purposes only and does not describe a real patient.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch2\u003EBackground\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cp\u003EA 68-year-old man with ischaemic cardiomyopathy, hypertension and type 2 diabetes attended a cardiology clinic two weeks after an admission for acute decompensated heart failure. His left ventricular ejection fraction was 30%. His medications were ramipril, low-dose bisoprolol, furosemide, aspirin, atorvastatin and metformin.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch2\u003EAssessment\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cp\u003EHe reported breathlessness when climbing one flight of stairs, consistent with NYHA class II to III symptoms, and had mild ankle oedema. His blood pressure was 108/68 mmHg, his heart rate was 82 beats per minute in sinus rhythm, his eGFR was 52 mL/min/1.73 m\u003Csup\u003E2\u003C/sup\u003E and his potassium was 4.6 mmol/L.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch2\u003EPlan\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cp\u003ETreatment was optimised in stages, with clinical and biochemical review after each change:\u003C/p\u003E\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EDapagliflozin and low-dose spironolactone were started, with furosemide reviewed for possible dose reduction.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EBlood tests were repeated at about 1 week and again at 4 weeks.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003ERamipril was stopped, and low-dose sacubitril/valsartan was started after a 36-hour washout.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EBisoprolol was gradually increased as heart rate and blood pressure allowed.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EHe was referred to cardiac rehabilitation and received education on daily weights and his action plan.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\u003Ch2\u003EOutcome\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cp\u003EAt 12 weeks, he had NYHA class II symptoms, no oedema and no further admissions. Repeat echocardiography showed an ejection fraction of 38%, so there was no current primary prevention indication for an implantable cardioverter-defibrillator. Ongoing follow-up was arranged.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch2\u003ELearning Points\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cp\u003EThe post-discharge period provides an opportunity to optimise treatment. Staged changes, close monitoring and coordinated care can help balance benefit against hypotension, renal impairment and hyperkalaemia.\u003C/p\u003E"
}]]></Value>
